shares stages 1-2 with plans/recap (enumerate the theory's own facts,
filter package noise). it is the SMALLER of the two: it reports a list,
not source, so it needs no restatement and no round-trip pre-flight —
recap's A1, the highest-risk assumption in that plan, does not exist
here. if both are built, build this one first: it settles recap's fact
FILTER (recap A2) and its trust-audit APIs (recap A3) on the way.

the name
--------

`proof_tasks`, NOT `task_list`. the host agent (claude code and
friends) has its own task tooling, and a tool called `task_list` served
over the same MCP connection reads as "the agent's todo list" rather
than "unproved obligations in a theory". the name says what the items
ARE. `sorries` was the other candidate — rejected as jargon that only
reads to an Isabelle user, and the tool's whole point is to drive an
agent that may not be one.

scala or ML?
------------

ML ONLY. no scala, same reasoning as plans/recap: enumerating a
theory's facts and inspecting derivations are prover-side operations
with no scala analogue, and registration through MCP_Tool.declare gets
tools/list for free. a scala builtin would still need an ML mirror row
AND still cross to ML for the work; the only thing it buys is being
listed when the context does not import the tools theory, which an
agent working a HOL session that already imports MCP-HOL does not need.

so: a new theory MCP_Task_List.thy in the MCP-HOL tree (next to
MCP_Repl.thy), an ML structure MCP_Task_List, one `mcp_tool`
declaration. it imports `MCP` (the HOL-anchored wrapper), never
`MCP-Tools.MCP_Tools` directly — see MCP.thy's own header for why a
Pure-based theory must not land as first parent.

input contract
--------------

  proof_tasks {theories: "Scheduler Tasks"} -> task list TEXT

theory names, resolved with Thy_Info.get_theory (the idiom entities /
find_theorems_theory already use, MCP_Repl.thy:101,159). an explicit
param, NOT the ambient tool_scope designation — same call as recap
made: the caller names the target rather than having to tool_scope_set
first.

THEORIES, not repls, for the reason plans/recap pins down: a repl's
transient facts exist in no file. a task list over a repl would list
obligations the client cannot act on by editing anything. refused, not
approximated.

what a task entry carries
-------------------------

  name       the externed fact name (Facts.extern against the theory's
             fact space) — the name a user would type
  position   file + line, from Name_Space.the_entry on
             Global_Theory.fact_space. this is the DECLARATION site and
             it is the idiom already in this tree (MCP_Repl.entities,
             MCP_Repl.thy:99-124). the oracle's own Position (the sorry
             site) is reported as a secondary hint when present and
             when it differs.
  statement  Thm.prop_of on the fact, printed in the theory's context.

the statement is NOT taken from the oracle's term. Skip_Proof.cheat_tac
(Pure/skip_proof.ML) builds its oracle over

  Logic.list_implies (map Thm.term_of assms, goal)

— the skipped SUBGOAL with the local assumptions prepended. for a
top-level `lemma foo: "P" sorry` that happens to equal the lemma; for a
sorry inside a structured proof (after fix/assume), or in a nested
have, it does not. using it would silently misreport the task.

mechanism
---------

1. enumerate: Facts.dest_static over Global_Theory.facts_of T with the
   parents' facts_of as exclusion bases — recap's step 1 verbatim.
     Facts.dest_static : bool -> Facts.T list -> Facts.T
                           -> (string * thm list) list   (Pure/facts.ML:40)
2. filter package noise, recap's step 2 — the datatype/fun output
   (foo.simps, foo.induct) must not appear as tasks. shared code with
   recap; whichever tool is built first owns it. Name_Space.is_concealed
   UNDER-filters; the kind tag (Thm.get_tags -> Markup.kindN) is the
   candidate, determined empirically (recap A2 / A3 here).
   NOTE: in practice a sorry'd package theorem is near-impossible, so
   this filter matters far less here than in recap — a wrong filter
   costs recap correctness, but costs this tool almost nothing. do not
   block on it.
3. select: keep the facts resting on the skip_proof oracle.
     Thm_Deps.has_skip_proof : thm list -> bool   (Pure/thm_deps.ML:35)
   which is all_oracles + a name test against \<^oracle_name>\<open>skip_proof\<close>.
4. classify DIRECT vs INHERITED (A1 — the one open question, see
   below). direct = the lemma itself is sorry'd, and is a real task.
   inherited = proved honestly but resting on a sorry'd lemma; not a
   task, it closes for free when its dependency closes. an unclassified
   list is still useful, so this step DEGRADES rather than blocks: if
   A1 comes out negative, emit one flat list and say in the output that
   direct/inherited could not be separated.
5. skip_proofs GATE, before anything is reported:
     Goal.skip_proofs_enabled ()   (Pure/Isar/proof.ML:1190)
   when true, every non-relevant proof carries the same oracle and the
   whole list is noise. the tool refuses with that sentence rather than
   returning a plausible wrong answer. (a stamped-but-returned variant
   was considered; refusing is the safer default and the caller can
   rebuild without quick_and_dirty. revisit if a real workflow needs
   the stamped form.)
6. assemble the text: one section per theory, tasks in source-position
   order (stable across calls — position order, not hash order), a
   count, and the inherited block last if step 4 produced one.

   (APIs verified 2026-08-12 against the bundled Isabelle2025-2:
    Thm_Deps.has_skip_proof / all_oracles — Pure/thm_deps.ML:35,23;
    the skip_proof oracle itself — Pure/skip_proof.ML:29;
    oracle = (string * Position.T) * term option — proofterm.ML:234;
    oracles recorded at the DEFAULT proof level: etc/options
      `record_proofs : int = -1` leaves Proofterm.proofs at 6
      (proofterm.ML:496) and oracle_enabled 6 = true (:494), so the
      position and term are present — thm.ML:1213. full proof terms
      are NOT needed. this settles what plans/recap listed as A3.)

name/argument mapping across the layers
---------------------------------------

  mcp        proof_tasks {theories}
  bridge     MCP.run_tool -> registry row "proof_tasks" (ml_run form)
  engine     MCP_Task_List.tasks : string list -> string

annotations: read_only. it reads a theory value and computes; it
mutates nothing and repeats identically.

implementation steps
--------------------

1. THE FIXTURE FIRST, before any tool code. mcp/Tools/HOL/Tests/
   MCP_Fixture_Sorry.thy carrying: a datatype, a fun, a definition, two
   real lemmas, one directly sorry'd lemma A, and one lemma B proved
   `using A by simp`. wire into mcp/Tools/ROOT under MCP-HOL-Tests.
   this single fixture settles A1 here AND recap's A2 filter question.
   [ml unit]
2. A1 EXPERIMENT on that fixture: does B's own proof body carry the
   oracle? assert both readings so the build records which one holds.
   the answer decides whether step 4 above ships or degrades. [ml unit]
3. enumeration + selection (mechanism 1-3): theory in, sorry'd facts
   out. [ml unit: exactly A (and B iff transitive), never the datatype
   or fun theorems]
4. entry assembly (name/position/statement) + the skip_proofs gate
   (mechanism 5). [ml unit: statement matches Thm.prop_of and NOT the
   oracle term for a structured-proof sorry; gate asserted]
5. text assembly + the `mcp_tool "proof_tasks" = run ...` declaration.
   [ml unit: golden text for the fixture]
6. bridge: proof_tasks over MCP.run_tool in a live session returns the
   list. [mcp_test -b]
7. close-out: CHANGELOG entry, plans/README entry, spec OPEN resolved
   with whatever A1 turned out to be.

ASSUMPTIONS -> tests   (ranked by risk)
---------------------------------------

- A1 DIRECT vs INHERITED [highest risk, and the only real unknown].
  Thm_Deps.all_oracles is transitive: it recurses the dependency
  closure (thm_deps.ML:23, `collect` folding over the PBody's thms), so
  a lemma proved honestly from a sorry'd one also reports skip_proof.
  the hypothesis is that a thm's OWN proof body holds only its own
  oracles, so `#oracles (Thm.proof_body_of thm)` discriminates. the
  hazard is fulfill_norm_proof (proofterm.ML:1999), which unions
  PROMISE oracles into exactly that field — so whether a cited lemma
  reads as direct or inherited may depend on whether it arrived as a
  promise or a thm_node, which depends on parallel_proofs. MCP-HOL
  builds with parallel proofs on.
  test: the step-1 fixture, `lemma A: "P" sorry` and
  `lemma B: "Q" using A by simp`; assert whether B's own body is empty
  of oracles. POSITIVE -> ship step 4 and blocked-by ordering.
  NEGATIVE -> fall back to Thm_Deps.thm_deps to walk named
  dependencies and intersect against the separately-computed direct
  set; if that also fails, degrade to one flat list (mechanism 4).
  do not describe the tool as having blocked-by ordering until this is
  green. [ml unit]
- A2 SKIP_PROOFS GATE actually fires. Goal.skip_proofs_enabled reads
  the ambient setting, so assert that a quick_and_dirty context makes
  the tool refuse rather than list every lemma in the fixture. this is
  the difference between a useful list and a silently meaningless one.
  [ml unit; may need MCP_Heap_Suite if the setting cannot be faked
  in-process]
- A3 STATEMENT SOURCE. for a sorry inside a structured proof
  (fix/assume, or a nested have), assert Thm.prop_of on the fact is
  what gets reported and that it DIFFERS from the oracle's term —
  i.e. the fixture actually exercises the divergence rather than
  passing by coincidence on a trivial case. [ml unit]
- A4 POSITION SURVIVES THE HEAP. the fact-space entry position is
  per-entry bookkeeping baked into the theory value at declaration
  time, so it should serialize (this is the same claim
  MCP_Repl.entities already relies on, plans/find_definition's
  entities notes). assert a task's file/line is non-empty when the
  theory comes from a saved heap rather than a fresh load.
  [MCP_Heap_Suite]
- A5 REFUSAL FOR REPLS, mirroring recap's A5: a repl id is not an
  accepted target and says why. [bridge]
